{
  // Root project, so VS Code has something to resolve against everywhere.
  //
  // Without this, any TypeScript outside apps/mobile and packages/contracts —
  // the shared package, both native modules — got no tsconfig at all. The
  // editor fell back to bare defaults with no module resolution and no library
  // types, and reported errors that `tsc` in CI never saw. Editor and CI
  // disagreeing about whether the code compiles is worse than either being
  // wrong on its own, because it teaches you to ignore the editor.
  //
  // References rather than one big program: each workspace keeps its own
  // settings (the app needs JSX and bundler resolution, the contracts need
  // CommonJS), and this only ties them together for the editor.
  "files": [],
  "references": [
    { "path": "./apps/mobile" },
    { "path": "./packages/contracts" },
    { "path": "./packages/shared" },
    { "path": "./modules/expo-tor" },
    { "path": "./modules/expo-zkprove" }
  ]
}
